So my aunt, who live in the same building, she saw this. She came down and started to kiss his boots. He should let me go. So he was-- I was lucky. He let me go. He did not take me to the police.

INT: Did your sisters have a bad experience?

JT: My sister had a very bad experience. My sister Eva, she was working on the train-- on the trains carrying coal. She was younger than I. She was carrying coal, and she was beaten constantly. But because she got the permit to work, she was temporarily saved from destruction.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JT: And my little sister, she-- she-- she also try. You-- you used to buy jobs with money to-- to-- in a factory, to sew the military buttons-- anything what cames-- clean the street. My mother was a-- cleaning office from a Gestapo-- just to protect, just get the permit to be alive.

INT: And how long did this continue?

JT: This continued the whole the time. Fortunately, my mother and father had a good wisdom. And they said, we cannot survive here as Jews, because they will-- slowly, they will kill us all.

So after the big akcja in 1941, in-- that was in '42, in-- I think it was September. I don't remember. But it was something-- September, August. They-- they used to round up us. And they had to--

INT: They used to-- I'm sorry.

JT: They round us up.

INT: Yes.

JT: They had to-- the Jewish Militsiya had to deliver something-- 100,000 Jews. So naturally, they wanted to save their skin. They used to go from house to house and collect the Jewish like you collect garbage.

We were hiding behind in a-- in a one apartment, which belonged to a Jewish [? politician, ?] a-- a-- and behind he camouflaged the room. He put some closets there. And we were 50 people in one room.

Naturally we were not children-- were young people or parents. So I, my mother, and my sister Lina, we were hiding there for two weeks. Two weeks we did not go out no place, or you stood without feet-- food, without nothing.

But fortunately, they never moved away the closet, and they did not find us. Because-- but we could hear the dogs going and sniffling and looking for the prey-- the Jewish prey. After this, my mother said-- and then I lost my aunt-- they took away. My cousin they took away.

So my mother said we cannot survive anymore as a Jew. Because, unfortunately, by us was not concentration camp. By us they used to take straight to Majdanek. They used to load the cattle wagons and ship right away to burn.

Or you-- or you were not-- we did not know about the forests, about the partisans. So we did not know that time yet. So my mother said, we have to try to survive different way. We have to pretend that we are not Jewish.

So my mother went to the principal of the school, because she knew him very well-- not to my school, the gymnasium, but to a-- a gentile gymnasium, because we were in the business, so she knew a lot of people. So he gave her a birth certificate. He gave her a diploma for me, and-- with my picture.

And my mother said, we have to go some other city. Naturally, my-- my aunt they took away. So my father felt, like, responsible for my cousin, who was a year older than I, and said, I have to go with her, to take her out to Warsaw, and try to settle her.

I was younger. Why? Because she was very semitic looks she had. And-- and he was afraid that if she will go, they will recognize. So I had to go. I had to take care of her. We came to Warsaw. And then we run away with the false papers.

